# banned-strings.list
#
# Truth-contract banned phrases for VitalCV public-facing surfaces. Any
# match in a non-allowlisted file fails CI.
#
# Lines starting with `#` are comments. Blank lines are ignored. Every
# other line is treated as a case-insensitive extended regex passed
# verbatim to `grep -iE`.
#
# To add a phrase: add the regex. To allow a file to contain it (e.g.
# a test asserting its absence, the policy doc itself, this very list):
# update `ALLOWLIST_GLOBS` in scripts/check-banned-strings.sh.
#
# See apps/web/CLAUDE.md for the authoritative truth contract.

# Compliance / certification overclaim
HIPAA compliant
SOC ?2 certified
NCQA certified
certified compliant

# Verification overclaim
automatically verified
guaranteed verification
final verification without review
source confirmed before response

# Credentialing overclaim
\bcomplete credentialing\b
instant credentialing

# Risk / acceptance overclaim
legally accepted
risk transferred

# Bare "Verified" label markers — match rendered JSX text and label /
# status attribute values. The quote class is a literal character set
# containing ASCII single + double quotes and the four common
# smart-quote glyphs (U+2018, U+2019, U+201C, U+201D) so a cleaned-up
# copy pass cannot hide a violation behind curly quotes. The phrase ban
# is specifically for the BARE word "Verified" used as a status —
# compound labels such as "Source-verified" remain allowed because the
# hyphen breaks the surrounding-quote anchor.
(label|status)\s*[:=]\s*['"‘’“”]Verified['"‘’“”]
>\s*Verified\s*<
